{"term": "هيام", "lang": "ar", "script": "arab", "romanization": "hayām", "category": "archaic_literary", "subcategory": "passionate_longing", "concept": "ardent_passion", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "literary", "crude": 8, "technical": 5, "recognition_pct_current": 88, "recognition_pct_peak": 92, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "Intense, consuming love-frenzy; the state of being utterly lost in passionate longing, to the point of emotional and mental wandering.", "english_term": "lovesickness; ardent longing", "english_translation_quality": "approximate", "notes": "Root هيم means to wander or roam aimlessly; classically describes the lover who loses all rational faculty to overwhelming desire; pervasive in classical Arabic poetry (especially Udhri love tradition); still widely understood in literary and lyrical contexts."}
{"term": "غرام", "lang": "ar", "script": "arab", "romanization": "gharām", "category": "archaic_literary", "subcategory": "infatuation", "concept": "amorous_desire", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "literary", "crude": 6, "technical": 5, "recognition_pct_current": 93, "recognition_pct_peak": 95, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "Deep, consuming passionate love or infatuation; an attachment so total it dominates the heart and will.", "english_term": "infatuation; passionate love", "english_translation_quality": "approximate", "notes": "Etymologically linked to gharama (debt/penalty), framing love as an inescapable obligation consuming the self; extremely common in song titles, classical poetry, and given names; remains current in modern Arabic lyric and literary use."}
{"term": "كلف", "lang": "ar", "script": "arab", "romanization": "kalaf", "category": "archaic_literary", "subcategory": "enamored_state", "concept": "amorous_feeling", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "archaic", "crude": 5, "technical": 5, "recognition_pct_current": 52, "recognition_pct_peak": 72, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "The state of being deeply enamored or besotted; an intense, consuming attachment to a beloved that marks the soul.", "english_term": "besotted; deeply enamored", "english_translation_quality": "approximate", "notes": "More archaic and less frequently used than غرام or هيام; primarily found in classical dictionaries and medieval poetry; root كلف also means blemish or skin discoloration, suggesting love as a stain that marks the lover permanently."}
{"term": "شبِق", "lang": "ar", "script": "arab", "romanization": "shabiq", "category": "desire_arousal_descriptors", "subcategory": "horny_descriptor", "concept": "lasciviousness", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "slang_neutral", "crude": 52, "technical": 10, "recognition_pct_current": 75, "recognition_pct_peak": 75, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "Lustful, lecherous; describes a person in a state of intense habitual sexual craving rather than momentary arousal.", "english_term": "lecherous; horny", "english_translation_quality": "approximate", "notes": "Typically applied to men; carries connotation of characterological lust rather than momentary arousal, which distinguishes it from هائج; appears in classical Arabic literature and persists in modern colloquial and MSA usage."}
{"term": "هائج", "lang": "ar", "script": "arab", "romanization": "hāʾij", "category": "desire_arousal_descriptors", "subcategory": "horny_descriptor", "concept": "sexual_arousal", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "slang_neutral", "crude": 55, "technical": 5, "recognition_pct_current": 92, "recognition_pct_peak": 92, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "Sexually aroused, agitated with desire; the primary and most ubiquitous colloquial Arabic word for 'horny.'", "english_term": "horny; sexually aroused", "english_translation_quality": "direct", "notes": "Root هاج means to be stirred up, agitated, or roused; widely used across MSA and all Arabic dialects with minimal regional variation; can also describe a riled animal or a rough sea, but in human contexts the sexual meaning is primary."}
{"term": "مشتهٍ", "lang": "ar", "script": "arab", "romanization": "mushtahī", "category": "desire_arousal_descriptors", "subcategory": "desire_state", "concept": "lust", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "slang_neutral", "crude": 28, "technical": 10, "recognition_pct_current": 82, "recognition_pct_peak": 82, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "Desiring, lusting, craving; in a state of strong appetite or yearning, especially sexual desire.", "english_term": "desiring; lusting after", "english_translation_quality": "approximate", "notes": "Derived from shahwa (appetite/desire), so applies equally to food and sexual craving; context disambiguates; softer connotation than شبِق, implying a momentary state rather than a characterological trait."}
{"term": "متحرق", "lang": "ar", "script": "arab", "romanization": "mutaḥarriq", "category": "desire_arousal_descriptors", "subcategory": "burning_desire", "concept": "ardent_passion", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "literary", "crude": 18, "technical": 5, "recognition_pct_current": 60, "recognition_pct_peak": 68, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "Burning with desire or longing; consumed by fervent, fiery yearning for someone.", "english_term": "burning with desire", "english_translation_quality": "approximate", "notes": "From ḥarīq (fire, burning); primarily poetic and literary metaphor for intense longing; less commonly heard in casual speech than هائج or شبِق; often paired with شوق (longing) in lyrical contexts."}
{"term": "محروم", "lang": "ar", "script": "arab", "romanization": "maḥrūm", "category": "desire_arousal_descriptors", "subcategory": "sexual_frustration", "concept": "sexual_desperation", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "slang_neutral", "crude": 32, "technical": 10, "recognition_pct_current": 93, "recognition_pct_peak": 93, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "Deprived; colloquially, sexually frustrated or starved of gratification; describes someone denied sexual access.", "english_term": "sexually deprived; sexually frustrated", "english_translation_quality": "approximate", "notes": "Primary meaning is 'deprived/forbidden' (from ḥarām = forbidden); in sexual contexts it is the single most common Arabic expression for sexual frustration or starvation; used with humor and seriousness across all Arabic dialects; محروم جنسياً is the explicit form."}
{"term": "جائع", "lang": "ar", "script": "arab", "romanization": "jāʾiʿ", "category": "desire_arousal_descriptors", "subcategory": "sexual_hunger_metaphor", "concept": "intense_sexual_craving", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "slang_neutral", "crude": 38, "technical": 5, "recognition_pct_current": 85, "recognition_pct_peak": 85, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "Literally 'hungry'; used as a sexual metaphor for someone ravenously craving sexual contact or intimacy.", "english_term": "starving (for sex)", "english_translation_quality": "approximate", "notes": "Standard Arabic word for hunger repurposed as a sexual metaphor; often extended in the phrase جائع جنسياً; the hunger metaphor for sexual craving is cross-cultural but is firmly established in colloquial Arabic across regions."}
{"term": "مولع", "lang": "ar", "script": "arab", "romanization": "mūlaʿ", "category": "desire_arousal_descriptors", "subcategory": "obsessive_infatuation", "concept": "ardent_passion", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "slang_neutral", "crude": 16, "technical": 5, "recognition_pct_current": 75, "recognition_pct_peak": 78, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "Consumed by passionate obsession; deeply infatuated and aflame with fixation on a person.", "english_term": "obsessively infatuated; consumed by passion", "english_translation_quality": "approximate", "notes": "From root ولع (to catch fire, to burn); implies an obsessive, consuming attachment rather than momentary arousal; bridges classical literary and modern colloquial Arabic; slightly more intense in connotation than عاشق (lover)."}
{"term": "متوقد", "lang": "ar", "script": "arab", "romanization": "mutawāqqid", "category": "desire_arousal_descriptors", "subcategory": "burning_desire", "concept": "ardent_passion", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "literary", "crude": 12, "technical": 5, "recognition_pct_current": 50, "recognition_pct_peak": 62, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "Blazing, kindled; describes desire or passion that is intensely lit and radiating emotional heat.", "english_term": "blazing with passion", "english_translation_quality": "approximate", "notes": "From وقد (to kindle or blaze); primarily poetic and literary; less common in everyday speech; evokes newly kindled or vividly active desire as opposed to a dying ember; often used of eyes, spirit, or passion in general."}
{"term": "شهواني", "lang": "ar", "script": "arab", "romanization": "shahwānī", "category": "desire_arousal_descriptors", "subcategory": "libidinous", "concept": "sensuality", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "slang_neutral", "crude": 52, "technical": 15, "recognition_pct_current": 87, "recognition_pct_peak": 87, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "Sensual, libidinous, driven by carnal desire; characterized by strong sexual appetite.", "english_term": "sensual; libidinous", "english_translation_quality": "approximate", "notes": "Adjective derived from shahwa (desire/lust/appetite); can describe a person, a work of art, a scene, or an atmosphere; widely used in both formal and colloquial MSA; higher crude score than its root shahwa because the adjectival form more directly labels someone as dominated by lust."}
{"term": "ناري", "lang": "ar", "script": "arab", "romanization": "nārī", "category": "desire_arousal_descriptors", "subcategory": "fiery_passion", "concept": "ardent_passion", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "slang_neutral", "crude": 26, "technical": 5, "recognition_pct_current": 83, "recognition_pct_peak": 83, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "Fiery; colloquially describes someone or something blazing with sexual passion or intense physical attractiveness.", "english_term": "fiery; blazing (with passion)", "english_translation_quality": "approximate", "notes": "Literally 'of fire' (nār = fire); versatile colloquial descriptor applied to passionate people, intense situations, and attractive individuals; not exclusively sexual but strongly implies sexual charge or intense allure in context."}
{"term": "بارد", "lang": "ar", "script": "arab", "romanization": "bārid", "category": "desire_arousal_descriptors", "subcategory": "sexual_coldness", "concept": "sexual_frigidity", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "slang_neutral", "crude": 12, "technical": 10, "recognition_pct_current": 93, "recognition_pct_peak": 93, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "Cold; in sexual contexts, sexually unresponsive, frigid, or indifferent to desire and arousal.", "english_term": "cold; frigid", "english_translation_quality": "approximate", "notes": "Primary meaning is temperature; the sexual meaning is widely understood across all Arabic-speaking communities; the most casual and commonly used Arabic term for a sexually cold partner; can apply to men or women but disproportionately applied to women in practice."}
{"term": "فاتر", "lang": "ar", "script": "arab", "romanization": "fātir", "category": "desire_arousal_descriptors", "subcategory": "tepid_desire", "concept": "low_libido", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "slang_neutral", "crude": 10, "technical": 12, "recognition_pct_current": 70, "recognition_pct_peak": 73, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "Lukewarm, tepid; describes diminished or mild enthusiasm, including reduced or weakened sexual desire.", "english_term": "lukewarm; tepid", "english_translation_quality": "approximate", "notes": "From فتر (to slacken or cool down); implies reduced rather than absent desire, making it milder than بارد; used across religious, emotional, and sexual contexts; فتور (the noun form) is the standard MSA term for sexual 'lukewarmness' or hypoactive desire."}
{"term": "بارد جنسياً", "lang": "ar", "script": "arab", "romanization": "bārid jinsiyyan", "category": "desire_arousal_descriptors", "subcategory": "sexual_coldness", "concept": "sexual_frigidity", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "clinical", "crude": 8, "technical": 68, "recognition_pct_current": 68, "recognition_pct_peak": 68, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "Literally 'sexually cold'; the standard MSA clinical compound phrase for sexual frigidity or complete absence of sexual responsiveness.", "english_term": "sexually frigid; sexually cold", "english_translation_quality": "direct", "notes": "The adverb jinsiyyan (sexually) makes this explicitly clinical rather than ambiguous; used in psychology, sexology journalism, and medical Arabic; the functional MSA equivalent of the clinical diagnosis of frigidity or hypoactive sexual desire disorder."}
{"term": "عديم الشهوة", "lang": "ar", "script": "arab", "romanization": "ʿadīm al-shahwa", "category": "desire_arousal_descriptors", "subcategory": "absence_of_desire", "concept": "low_libido", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "clinical", "crude": 5, "technical": 60, "recognition_pct_current": 62, "recognition_pct_peak": 62, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "Devoid of desire; literally 'lacking shahwa'; the MSA clinical expression for complete absence of sexual libido.", "english_term": "lacking sexual desire; zero libido", "english_translation_quality": "approximate", "notes": "ʿadīm = devoid/lacking; al-shahwa = the desire/lust; a transparent clinical descriptor rather than a single lexical item; also applicable to absent appetite for food; the most analytically direct MSA phrase for clinical anaphrodisia."}
{"term": "خامد", "lang": "ar", "script": "arab", "romanization": "khāmid", "category": "desire_arousal_descriptors", "subcategory": "extinguished_desire", "concept": "sexual_frigidity", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "literary", "crude": 10, "technical": 10, "recognition_pct_current": 62, "recognition_pct_peak": 68, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "Extinguished, subdued; describes passion or desire that has gone cold or been completely quenched.", "english_term": "extinguished (desire); spent", "english_translation_quality": "approximate", "notes": "From خمد (to be extinguished); a fire metaphor implying desire that was once present but has now died out; primarily literary; used for dampened spirits, suppressed rebellion, and exhausted passion alike; implies prior existence of desire now gone."}
{"term": "خاملة", "lang": "ar", "script": "arab", "romanization": "khāmila", "category": "desire_arousal_descriptors", "subcategory": "sexual_inertia", "concept": "sexual_frigidity", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "slang_neutral", "crude": 18, "technical": 10, "recognition_pct_current": 68, "recognition_pct_peak": 68, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "Inactive, sluggish, inert; feminine adjective used to describe a sexually passive or unresponsive woman.", "english_term": "sexually inert; sexually passive", "english_translation_quality": "approximate", "notes": "Feminine of خامل (lazy/inert); general meaning is sluggishness or inactivity; applied to women it implies sexual passivity or complete lack of initiative and enthusiasm; carries a mildly pejorative connotation in sexual contexts."}
{"term": "جامدة", "lang": "ar", "script": "arab", "romanization": "jāmida", "category": "desire_arousal_descriptors", "subcategory": "sexual_rigidity", "concept": "sexual_frigidity", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "slang_neutral", "crude": 38, "technical": 10, "recognition_pct_current": 75, "recognition_pct_peak": 75, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "Frozen, rigid, unresponsive; feminine adjective for a completely sexually frigid or wholly inert woman.", "english_term": "frigid; stiff", "english_translation_quality": "approximate", "notes": "Feminine of جامد (frozen/solid/rigid); stronger and more contemptuous than خاملة; implies total absence of sexual feeling or arousal response; a commonly deployed colloquial insult for a sexually unresponsive woman; higher crude score than other frigidity terms due to its insulting edge."}
{"term": "لا تستجيب", "lang": "ar", "script": "arab", "romanization": "lā tastajīb", "category": "desire_arousal_descriptors", "subcategory": "sexual_unresponsiveness", "concept": "sexual_frigidity", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "slang_neutral", "crude": 18, "technical": 15, "recognition_pct_current": 70, "recognition_pct_peak": 70, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "Does not respond; colloquial phrase for a woman who fails to react to sexual stimulation or show any sign of arousal.", "english_term": "unresponsive", "english_translation_quality": "approximate", "notes": "A phrase rather than a single lexeme; the feminine verb form (tastajīb) marks this as gendered toward describing women; used descriptively to denote failure of arousal response; less openly insulting in tone than جامدة; implies a physiological or psychological block more than a character flaw."}
{"term": "مثلي الجنس", "lang": "ar", "script": "arab", "romanization": "mithlī al-jins", "category": "identities_orientations", "subcategory": "homosexual_identity", "concept": "homosexual", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "clinical", "crude": 15, "technical": 75, "recognition_pct_current": 92, "recognition_pct_peak": 92, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "Homosexual; the standard Modern Standard Arabic clinical and journalistic term for a person attracted to the same sex.", "english_term": "homosexual", "english_translation_quality": "direct", "notes": "Literally 'same-sex [person]'; a calque from Western clinical terminology; dominant in MSA journalism, law, and public health contexts; gender-neutral in form; preferred in formal writing over colloquial slurs; heavily stigmatized as a topic in most Arabic-speaking societies despite the clinical register of the term itself."}
{"term": "مثلية جنسية", "lang": "ar", "script": "arab", "romanization": "mithliyya jinsiyya", "category": "identities_orientations", "subcategory": "homosexuality_orientation", "concept": "homosexual", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "clinical", "crude": 15, "technical": 78, "recognition_pct_current": 85, "recognition_pct_peak": 85, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "Homosexuality; the abstract MSA noun for same-sex sexual orientation as a concept or phenomenon.", "english_term": "homosexuality", "english_translation_quality": "approximate", "notes": "Abstract noun form distinguishing the orientation concept from مثلي الجنس (the person); mithliyya = sameness + jinsiyya = sexual; rated approximate rather than direct because 'homosexuality' maps the orientation concept while this term straddles orientation and identity as used in Arabic academic, medical, and legal discourse."}
{"term": "سحاقية", "lang": "ar", "script": "arab", "romanization": "saḥāqiyya", "category": "identities_orientations", "subcategory": "lesbian_identity", "concept": "lesbian", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "clinical", "crude": 30, "technical": 38, "recognition_pct_current": 82, "recognition_pct_peak": 82, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "Lesbian; a woman sexually and romantically attracted to other women; derived etymologically from the verb meaning to grind or rub.", "english_term": "lesbian", "english_translation_quality": "direct", "notes": "Derived from سحاق (grinding/rubbing), referencing the sexual act of tribadism; higher crude score than مثلي الجنس because the etymological root is a sexual act rather than a neutral descriptor; used in both clinical and everyday Arabic but carries the weight of its anatomical etymology in ways the English 'lesbian' does not."}
{"term": "سحاق", "lang": "ar", "script": "arab", "romanization": "saḥāq", "category": "identities_orientations", "subcategory": "tribadism_lesbianism", "concept": "tribadism", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "clinical", "crude": 32, "technical": 45, "recognition_pct_current": 82, "recognition_pct_peak": 82, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "Lesbianism; etymologically refers to tribadism (vulva-to-vulva sexual friction); the root noun from which سحاقية is derived, used for both the sexual act and lesbian orientation.", "english_term": "lesbianism; tribadism", "english_translation_quality": "approximate", "notes": "From the verb سحق (to grind, crush, or rub); historically denoted the sexual act specifically but has generalized to denote lesbian identity and orientation; the Arabic cognate of the clinical English term 'tribadism'; retains more of its act-based etymology than سحاقية, which is why it maps primarily to tribadism despite its broader usage."}
